{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015knZVf12AnhjcsCsNid8KL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the experiment plan. Let me identify which experiments are in \"planned\" or \"running\" status and need predictions. Looking at the registry and experiment plan:\n\n**RUNNING experiments (need predictions):**\n1. `70b_docred_unconstrained` (P030)\n2. `70b_docred_constrained` (P030)\n3. `p8b_docred_latency` (P035)\n\n**DONE experiments that already have results** — most of the plan experiments already have results.\n\nLet me re-read the experiment plan more carefully. \n\nMost experiments in the plan already have results filled in, so they're complete. The ones still in progress or planned are P030 (70B DocRED, which is currently running), P031 (error analysis with no registry entry yet), P032 (EE FT composability as a priority 2 planned experiment), and P033 (grammar-only cross-model validation, which appears to be cut off).\n\nWait, I need to reconsider what the user is actually asking for. They're requesting predictions for every planned experiment in the plan, but the distinction between \"planned\" and \"already done\" matters here. Some experiments are complete with results, while others are still in progress or haven't started yet. The user might want me to either predict outcomes for incomplete experiments or validate/retrodict for the ones already done, depending on how they're interpreting \"planned.\"\n\nLooking at the actual status of experiments in the registry, I can see which ones are still running versus which have finished. The \"best\" tier scenario seems to be about projecting what the in-flight experiments will show if everything goes optimally. Let me focus on identifying which experiments are still active and need predictions. I see the user wants a complete experimental results package—actual data for finished experiments and predictions for the remaining ones that